nuko-ime model-pipeline — Data Source Notice
=============================================

This pipeline produces statistical language models and a system dictionary
for the libakaza-based conversion engine of nuko-ime. The generated files
(SKK-JISYO.akaza, unigram.model, bigram.model, skip_bigram.model) are
derivative works of the following data sources.

The pipeline scripts (Makefile, scripts/, dict/) in this directory are
licensed under MIT, consistent with the rest of nuko-ime.

The generated model files distributed in GitHub Releases inherit the
following licenses from their data sources:

1. Japanese Wikipedia (via akaza-corpus-stats)
----------------------------------------------
Source: https://dumps.wikimedia.org/
License: Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International
         (CC BY-SA 4.0)
         https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/
Copyright: Wikimedia Foundation and contributors

Word frequency statistics aggregated from Japanese Wikipedia text are
included in the generated unigram/bigram/skip_bigram models. As a
derivative work, redistribution is subject to CC BY-SA 4.0 terms.

2. Aozora Bunko (青空文庫) (via akaza-corpus-stats)
---------------------------------------------------
Source: https://www.aozora.gr.jp/
License: Public Domain

Text from copyright-expired Japanese literary works was used to compute
word frequency statistics.

3. CC-100 Japanese (via akaza-corpus-stats)
-------------------------------------------
Source: https://data.statmt.org/cc-100/
Paper: Conneau et al., "Unsupervised Cross-lingual Representation Learning
       at Scale", ACL 2020. https://aclanthology.org/2020.lrec-1.494/

Aggregated word counts from the Japanese portion of CC-100 are included
in the statistical models.

4. UniDic
---------
Source: https://clrd.ninjal.ac.jp/unidic/
License: BSD-3-Clause / GPL-2.0 dual license (nuko-ime selects BSD-3-Clause)
Copyright: National Institute for Japanese Language and Linguistics (NINJAL)

UniDic CSV is used to populate the vocabulary of SKK-JISYO.akaza.
nuko-ime distributes derivative work under the BSD-3-Clause terms of
the dual license.

5. SudachiDict
--------------
Source: https://github.com/WorksApplications/SudachiDict
License: Apache License, Version 2.0
         https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0
Copyright: Works Applications Co., Ltd.

SudachiDict CSV (small_lex / core_lex / notcore_lex) supplements proper
noun entries in SKK-JISYO.akaza.

6. IPADIC (via Vibrato)
-----------------------
Source: https://github.com/daac-tools/vibrato
License: BSD-3-Clause (IPADIC), Apache-2.0 (Vibrato)

IPADIC is used as the tokenizer dictionary during corpus statistics
generation (in akaza-corpus-stats). The dictionary data itself is not
included in the generated models.

7. akaza-corpus-stats Pipeline
------------------------------
Source: https://github.com/akaza-im/akaza-corpus-stats
License: MIT (pipeline scripts)

The pipeline scripts of akaza-corpus-stats are MIT-licensed; the data
they produce inherits the licenses of items 1-3 and 6 above.

8. Seed Dictionary (dict/SKK-JISYO.akaza)
------------------------------------------
Source (Phase 2-B): derived from akaza-im/akaza@8a40428 default-model/dict/
                    https://github.com/akaza-im/akaza
License: MIT

In Phase 2-E this seed will be replaced with nuko-ime's own entries
maintained under the same MIT terms.

9. Training Corpus (training-corpus/{must,should,may}.txt)
-----------------------------------------------------------
Source (Phase 2-C): derived from akaza-im/akaza@8a40428 default-model/training-corpus/
                    https://github.com/akaza-im/akaza
License: Public Domain (per upstream README.md)

These small text corpora (~125 KB total) provide weak/medium/strong
conversion hints for learn-corpus. nuko-ime may extend them in Phase 2-E.
